      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "Copyright © 2016 Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
            "Label": "BACKGROUND AND OBJECTIVES",
            "content": "Gastric antral vascular ectasia is a relatively common endoscopic finding. Past studies have shown an association of gastric antral vascular ectasia with cirrhosis and autoimmune disorders. We aimed to re-examine these associations and to investigate a possible association of gastric antral vascular ectasia with features of the metabolic syndrome."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "METHODS",
            "content": "There were 135 patients with a diagnosis of gastric antral vascular ectasia from years 1995-2013 seen at the University of Virginia who were identified from a clinical data repository and age and sex matched to a cohort of patients without gastric antral vascular ectasia undergoing endoscopy within the same time frame as the index cases. The groups were compared for comorbidities including autoimmune disease, cirrhosis, vascular disease, body mass index (BMI), diabetes mellitus, and cirrhosis due to nonalcoholic steatohepatitis."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "Sixty-four percent of gastric antral vascular ectasia patients were cirrhotic, compared with 14% of controls (P <.001). Vascular disease was more common in the gastric antral vascular ectasia cohort (57% vs 36%; P <.001). The mean BMI was also higher in the gastric antral vascular ectasia cohort (33.7 kg/m(2) vs 28.8 kg/m(2); P <.001). Diabetes mellitus and nonalcoholic steatohepatitis cirrhosis were more frequently observed in gastric antral vascular ectasia subjects (64% vs 29% in controls [P <.001] and 28% vs 2% [P <.001], respectively). There was not an increased prevalence of autoimmune disease in gastric antral vascular ectasia patients vs controls (15% vs 13%; P = .861)."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSION",
            "content": "These results confirm the association of gastric antral vascular ectasia with underlying cirrhosis and revealed a significant correlation of gastric antral vascular ectasia with features of metabolic syndrome such as diabetes, BMI, vascular disease, and nonalcoholic steatohepatitis cirrhosis. The pathophysiology of gastric antral vascular ectasia remains uncertain, but we speculate that it may be a manifestation of the metabolic syndrome."
          }
        ]
      },
